<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to generic type parameters

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.GenericTypes-->
<docs:GenericTypes xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:def="org.blockml.bml.definition">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to generic type parameters
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: generic type parameters, declaring genericTypes, concrete application, and the
    bind-or-forward rule, grounded in org.blockml.bml.documentation.GenericsModel and
    org.blockml.bml.definition.GenericTypeDefinition. Body content is authored in a03 from those
    sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:GenericsModel" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — declaration, binding, forwarding, and instance bml:generics.
    </see>
    <see type="def:GenericTypeDefinition" relation="Requires">
      Meta-model for one genericTypes parameter entry.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:TypeExpressions" relation="SeeAlso">
      Generic application as a type expression.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Generic Types</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="What a generic type parameter is">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A generic type parameter lets a Block declare a placeholder type that concrete uses
of that Block fill in later, so one definition can serve many concrete type
combinations. A generic parameter belongs exclusively to the Block that declares it —
it is never inherited implicitly.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Declaring a generic parameter">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A generic parameter's name and base-type constraint are declared once, directly on
the Block that introduces it. Every concrete argument supplied later, wherever that
Block is used, must satisfy the declared constraint.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Binding or forwarding every parameter">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>When a Block extends a generic Block, it must account for every one of that Block's
parameters — either binding it to one concrete type or forwarding it as a parameter of
its own — so no parameter is ever silently dropped. Resolution happens during
inheritance: once a parameter is bound, that concrete type is substituted everywhere
the parameter appeared, so every parameter must eventually ground to something
concrete.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Applying a generic type concretely">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A concrete application supplies a type argument for each of a generic Block's
parameters, producing one fully concrete type with nothing generic left behind.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:CodeBlock>
              <code>&lt;type:IntegerEntity&gt;
  &lt;baseType&gt;
    core:PrimitiveEntity(type:Integer)
  &lt;/baseType&gt;
&lt;/type:IntegerEntity&gt;</code>
            </dpage:CodeBlock>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with Members">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Members" summary="Move from types themselves into the members that use them — Properties, Aggregations, and Associations.">
                  <target>docs:Members</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:GenericTypes>
